      "id": 2,
      "claim": "Chlorophyll is the pigment that gives leaves their green color; higher concentrations produce uniformly deep green foliage.",
      "evidence": "Plant physiology texts state that chlorophyll absorbs red and blue light and reflects green, and its concentration correlates with leaf darkness.",
      "weight": 0.10,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Many popular hosta cultivars have reduced chlorophyll in parts of the leaf, creating variegated, gold, or blue‑gray foliage that is highly prized.",
      "evidence": "Cultivar descriptions such as ‘Patriot’ (white margins), ‘June’ (gold), and ‘Halcyon’ (blue‑gray) note lower chlorophyll levels and are repeatedly awarded by the Hosta Society of America.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "For these cultivars, the aesthetic optimum – i.e., looking their best – is the clear expression of their characteristic non‑green color pattern, not maximal greenness.",
      "evidence": "Award criteria from the Hosta Society and garden shows emphasize the distinctness of variegation or hue; plants that lose those traits are judged lower.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [1, 3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Excessive chlorophyll would mask or diminish those patterns, turning a variegated or colored hosta into a plain green leaf.",
      "evidence": "Horticultural guides warn that “green‑out” occurs when chlorophyll production increases (e.g., due to too much sun), causing loss of variegation and reducing ornamental value.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [2, 3, 4]
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "claim": "Therefore, hostas do not need lots of chlorophyll to look their best; appropriate (often reduced) chlorophyll levels that allow the desired foliage pattern are sufficient.",
      "evidence": "Combining steps 1‑5 shows that aesthetic quality depends on color pattern, not on high chlorophyll content, and many award‑winning cultivars thrive with lower chlorophyll.",
